ainty: A Conceptual Basis for Uncertainty Management in Model-Based Decision Support\" (*Integrated Assessment*)\n- **Lempert, Popper & Bankes (2003)** – *Shaping the Next One Hundred Years* (RAND) — the canonical \"Robust Decision Making\" framework\n- **Ben-Haim (2006)** – *Info-Gap Decision Theory* (Academic Press)\n- **Marchau et al. (2019)** – *Decision Making under Deep Uncertainty* (Springer Open — freely available online)\n\n### 2. The Mathematical Concepts in the \"Excerpt\" Are Coherent but Unverified\nThe formalization described — a policy envelope as a mapping E: I(t) → Δ(D) from an information set to a simplex over decision space, updated via Bayesian filtering — is a real and recognizable structure in decision theory. **But I cannot confirm it appears in this book, because I cannot confirm this book exists.**\n\n### 3. The Netherlands Flood Case Study Context Is Real\nThe IJsselmeer/Afsluitdijk management problem, Rijkswaterstaat, the KNMI climate scenarios, and the Water Act of 2009 are all real. The case is genuinely used in adaptive policy literature. **But the specific analysis attributed to Chapter 5 is unverified.**\n\n---\n\n## Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Check MIT Press directly**: [mitpress.mit.edu](https://mitpress.mit.edu) — search the catalog for this title and ISBN.\n2. **Check WorldCat**: [worldcat.org](https://worldcat.org) — search by ISBN `978-0-26203-847-1`.\n3. **Check your university library catalog** — if it appears there, the book is real and you can request it.\n4. **Search Google Scholar** for \"Papadimitriou Nishihara stochastic governance\" or \"adaptive policy envelope\" to find verifiable publications.\n5. **Contact MIT Policy Press** directly to confirm the title exists.\n\nIf the book **does** exist and you can share a confirmed library link or ISBN that resolves cleanly, I'm happy to re-run retrieval against a verified source. If it turns out the book **doesn't** exist, I can help you build an equivalent seminar around